{"id":4426,"date":"2021-12-06T15:25:15","date_gmt":"2021-12-06T23:25:15","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=4426"},"modified":"2025-09-02T09:04:01","modified_gmt":"2025-09-02T16:04:01","slug":"4-1-user-input-cde-2","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/4-1-user-input-cde-2\/","title":{"rendered":"4.1: User Input"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213bf9ed41\" name=\"6a2213bf9ed41\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Before You Fly<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text css=&#8221;.vc_custom_1730227373096{background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}&#8221;]<\/p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\" wp-image-11766 alignleft\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/06\/icon_warning-150x150-2-84x85.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"106\" height=\"107\" \/><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\"><strong>PRE-FLIGHT WARNING<\/strong><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\">Our drones are durable, yet not indestructible. Please exercise caution while piloting your drone. To prolong the longevity of your drone, avoid flying into walls or other objects. Prior to takeoff, please review lesson <em>0.2 \u201cBefore You Fly\u201d.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213bf9ee79\" name=\"6a2213bf9ee79\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Goals and Steps to Success<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text css=&#8221;.vc_custom_1724801201939{padding-top: 15px !important;padding-right: 15px !important;padding-bottom: 15px !important;padding-left: 15px !important;background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}&#8221;]<img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"size-medium wp-image-11930 alignleft\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2022\/06\/product_brand-300x85.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"300\" height=\"85\" data-wp-editing=\"1\" srcset=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2022\/06\/product_brand-300x85.png 300w, https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2022\/06\/product_brand.png 729w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px\" \/><\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><em>This lesson currently is not optimized for \u201cPython for Robolink\u201d. Results may vary for console input functions.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]Goals:<\/p>\n<p>By the end of this lesson, I will be able to:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Identify the importance of input in programs<\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Create and run programs that include user interaction<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Store and modify user inputs<\/span><\/li>\n<li>Utilize user input to make decisions in a program<\/li>\n<li>Allow user input to change program flow during flight<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Steps to Success:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Input<\/li>\n<li>Dizzy Drone Activity<\/li>\n<li>Creating a Menu<\/li>\n<li>Make Your Own Menu<\/li>\n<li>Resetting and User Input<\/li>\n<li>Making Decisions<\/li>\n<li>Final Code<\/li>\n<li>Challenge: Custom Polygon<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213bf9eee7\" name=\"6a2213bf9eee7\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Input<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Up to this point, you cannot interact with the code or change its behavior while it is running. The code that you write will run exactly the same way each time. In this lesson, you will learn how to affect the output of the code using the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">input()<\/code> function. The\u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">input()<\/code> function is a way for the user to assign variables while the program is running.[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]The <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">input()<\/code> function is actually pretty simple, it looks like this:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">stored_value = input(\"Enter a value to save\")<\/pre>\n<p>What you will notice is that we always save the input to a variable. The only parameter it has is a string that it uses to prompt the user to enter a question. To see this in action, watch the video below and follow along:<\/p>\n<p>https:\/\/youtu.be\/ou1ey3BIk2E[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213bf9ef77\" name=\"6a2213bf9ef77\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Dizzy Drone Activity<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Before we get started with the dizzy drone activity, we need to understand a little more about how the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">input()<\/code> function works and what type of value it is actually giving us.<\/p>\n<p><iframe loading=\"lazy\" title=\"User Input Part 2\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" src=\"https:\/\/www.youtube.com\/embed\/SWF-VzrS1HA?feature=oembed&#038;enablejsapi=1&#038;origin=https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share\" referrerpolicy=\"strict-origin-when-cross-origin\" allowfullscreen><\/iframe><\/p>\n<p>To recap:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>The input function gives us a string.<\/li>\n<li>If we want to change the string to an integer, we use the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">int(value)<\/code> function<\/li>\n<li>We can tell what data type a variable is by using the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">type(value)<\/code> function<\/li>\n<li>To make our program more user-friendly, we need to make sure the prompt asks for the correct types of information<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]Now that we have an understanding of how to use the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">input()<\/code> function, let&#8217;s try an example.<\/p>\n<p>We want to write a program that tells the drone to spin around a set number of times. The number of times it will spin around is based on user input from the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">input()<\/code> function.<\/p>\n<p>The code should look like:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">import time\r\n\r\nfrom codrone_edu.drone import *\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n# Ask user how many times to spin around\r\nnum_spins = int(input(\"How many times should the drone spin:\"))\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\ndrone.hover(1)\r\n\r\ndrone.set_yaw(50)\r\ni = 0\r\nwhile i &lt; num_spins: # start counting at 0\r\n    drone.move(5)\r\n    i += 1\r\n\r\ndrone.land()\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213bf9efd2\" name=\"6a2213bf9efd2\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Creating a Menu<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">One of the best ways to understand the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">input()<\/code> function in action is by creating a <strong>menu<\/strong> of flight movements. A menu is a list of options to select from. You have probably selected a meal before from a restaurant menu or selected a character from a video game screen. Menus allow the user to select a particular outcome. In programming, this means that your program can now handle lots of different cases.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In this lesson, you will be writing a program that will ask a user to select a flight sequence and then send the right commands to the drone.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;7988&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213bf9f038\" name=\"6a2213bf9f038\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Make Your Own Menu<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><em>Ready to make your own?<\/em> Import the \u201ctime\u201d library. Next, set up a while loop so that while true, the menu will be printed.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Inside the while loop, include print commands for a \u201cMenu\u201d and then options for different maneuvers that you would like your CoDrone EDU to do. This can be anything, but you should have an idea of how to program each maneuver you include! Make sure one of those options is \u201cQuit\u201d, which will end the while loop.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your menu should look something like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\r\n\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\nwhile True:\r\n\r\n    print(\"Menu: \")\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 print(\"\"\"1. Spin Around\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a02. Fly in a triangle\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a03. Fly in a square\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a04. Sway\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a05. Quit\"\"\")<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Wondering why we use triple quotes instead of singular quotes<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">? Putting triple quotes allows you to create a string that span multiple lines. Single quotes won&#8217;t be able to enclose multi-line text. To see how it works, try replacing triple quotes with single quotes.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213bf9f0a9\" name=\"6a2213bf9f0a9\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Resetting and User Input<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your program needs to be able to read user input and determine which set of code to execute. You will use if and else if statements to check if the user input is 1,2,3,4 or 5.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Make sure you\u2019re still in the while loop, and then set your drone\u2019s roll, pitch, yaw, and throttle to 0 and use the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">move(0.5)<\/code> function after<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">. That way, they will all be ready for each maneuver you program! Next, create a variable called <\/span><strong>option<\/strong><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> to store <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">input()<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">. In the parameters for <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">input()<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">, <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">write<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> \u201cSelect an option: &#8220;. <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">This will print the command, and when the user inputs a number, it will be stored as an option and called later on.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">This is what you should have now:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">drone.set_throttle(0)\r\n\r\ndrone.set_roll(0)\r\n\r\ndrone.set_yaw(0)\r\n\r\ndrone.set_pitch(0)\r\n\r\ndrone.move(0.5)\r\n\r\noption = int(input(\"Select an option: \u201c))<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213bf9f124\" name=\"6a2213bf9f124\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Making Decisions<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Depending on the user input, your CoDrone will fly a different pattern! The best way to set this up is with conditionals. Have your drone take off, and then for option 1, set up an if statement and include your drone\u2019s maneuver.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">    if option == 1:\r\n        drone.takeoff()\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    drone.set_yaw(30)\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.move(2)<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">For options 2 through 4, use else if statements, and then include drone maneuvers.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">    elif option == 2:\r\n        drone.takeoff()\r\n        drone.triangle()\r\n\r\n    elif option == 3:\r\n        drone.takeoff()\r\n        drone.square()\r\n\r\n    elif option == 4:\r\n        drone.takeoff()\r\n        drone.sway()<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">For option 5, use another else if statement again, but include a print command that says, \u201cYou have quit the menu\u201d and then a break. This will exit the loop.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">    elif option == 5:\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    print(\"You have quit the menu.\")\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 break<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Next, include an else statement that will work when an invalid user input (like a number outside 1-5) is entered. This is not necessary, but it is good programming practice to provide feedback to the user when they have input something incorrect.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">    else:\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    print(\"Not a valid input. Try again.\")<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Finally, have your drone land and then (this is optional) include a print command that says \u201cDone.\u201d<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">    drone.land()\r\nprint(\"Done\")<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213bf9f17c\" name=\"6a2213bf9f17c\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 9<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Final Code<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your final program should look something like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\r\n\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\nwhile True:\r\n\r\n    print(\"Menu: \")\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 print(\"\"\"1. Spin Around\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a02. Fly in a triangle\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a03. Fly in a square\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a04. Sway\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a05. Quit\"\"\")\r\n\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_throttle(0)\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_roll(0)\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_yaw(0)\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_pitch(0)\r\n    drone.move(0.5)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 option = input(\"Select an option: \")\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 if option == \"1\":\r\n        drone.takeoff()\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    drone.set_yaw(30)\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.move(2)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 elif option == \"2\":\r\n        drone.takeoff()\r\n        drone.triangle()\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 elif option == \"3\":\r\n\r\n        drone.takeoff()\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    drone.square()\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 elif option == \"4\":\r\n\r\n        drone.takeoff()\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.sway()\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 elif option == \"5\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 print(\"You have quit the menu.\")\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 break\r\n\r\n    else:\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 print(\"Not a valid input. Try again.\")\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.land()\r\n\r\nprint(\"Done\")\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213bf9f1df\" name=\"6a2213bf9f1df\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 10<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge: Custom Polygon<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]For this challenge, you will need to create a program that does the following:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Takes in a value between 2 and 6 (2 is a circle)<\/li>\n<li>Prints out a menu with each value option<\/li>\n<li>Uses that value to make the appropriate sided polygon (for example, 4 would make the drone fly in a square)<\/li>\n<li>Takeoff before requesting user input<\/li>\n<li>Land once the polygon is completed.<\/li>\n<li>Use at least one sequence in the program.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>It is strongly recommended that you go back to the for loops lesson and use your polygon program that you have already written and modify it to fit these new requirements. (We call this\u00a0<strong>code reuse<\/strong>, and it is a popular concept in the professional world of programming)[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213bf9f225\" name=\"6a2213bf9f225\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 11<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Lesson Complete<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]In this lesson, you learned about user input! User input is important for programs that rely on user interaction and allowing users to make decisions in a program.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;7546&#8243; img_size=&#8221;750&#215;670&#8243; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 12 Before You Fly [vc_column_text css=\".vc_custom_1730227373096{background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}\"] PRE-FLIGHT WARNING Our drones are durable, yet not indestructible. Please exercise caution while piloting your drone. To prolong&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/4-1-user-input-cde-2\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":10822,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":3875,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1782,1848,1793,1826,1482,853,694,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[4328,14401,2121,2122,2134,2136],"class_list":["post-4426","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-cde","lesson-tag-cdej","lesson-tag-keyboard","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","lesson-tag-user-input","module-expert","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4426","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":47,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4426\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":13527,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4426\/revisions\/13527"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/10822"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=4426"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=4426"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=4426"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}